        """The foundational pillar of student retention literature is Vincent Tinto's (1975, 1993) Student Integration Model. Tinto posited that college departure is primarily a longitudinal process of reciprocal interactions between the individual student and the academic and social systems of the higher education institution. Tinto argued that students enter universities with diverse background characteristics, secondary school achievements, and family commitments. As they navigate campus life, their degree of academic integration (measured by cognitive development, academic performance, and intellectual congruence with faculty) and social integration (measured by peer group interactions, extracurricular involvement, and a subjective sense of belonging) continuously reshape their institutional commitment and goal commitment. When integration fails across both dimensions, voluntary departure or academic dismissal inevitably ensues.""",
