        """To overcome the variance of single decision trees while retaining the capacity to capture intricate non-linear decision surfaces, ensemble machine learning architectures emerged as the preeminent state-of-the-art for tabular educational records.""",
        """Leo Breiman (2001) introduced Random Forests, an ensemble paradigm based on bootstrap aggregation (bagging). A Random Forest constructs a diverse collection of randomized decision trees, each trained on an independent bootstrap sample drawn with replacement from the training dataset. At each candidate split node, the algorithm considers only a randomly selected subset of features (typically the square root of total features). By averaging the posterior probabilities across hundreds of decorrelated decision trees, Random Forests dramatically depress model variance without increasing bias. In educational survey modeling, this bagging mechanism provides exceptional robustness against label noise and subjective Likert-scale response variances.""",
        """Concurrently, Jerome Friedman (2001) developed Gradient Boosted Decision Trees (GBDT). Unlike bagging, boosting operates sequentially: each successive decision tree is trained to predict the negative gradient (pseudo-residuals) of the loss function associated with the preceding ensemble. By iteratively correcting classification errors and minimizing binomial log-likelihood loss, gradient boosting systematically drives down model bias in complex, overlapping decision boundaries. When regularized via shrinkage rates (learning rate eta) and shallow tree depth constraints, GBDT achieves peak discriminative power on structured tabular data.""",
        """Extensive empirical surveys (e.g., Grinsztajn et al., 2022; Shwartz-Ziv & Armon, 2022) across hundreds of tabular benchmarks confirm that tree-based ensembles consistently outperform deep neural networks (such as multi-layer perceptrons, TabNet, and transformers) on heterogeneous tabular datasets with under 50,000 instances. Deep learning architectures require extensive hyperparameter tuning, lack spatial inductive biases on un-ordered tabular columns, and remain prone to severe overfitting, whereas tree ensembles provide optimal computational efficiency, sub-second inference speed, and superior area under the ROC curve (ROC-AUC).""",
